+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[5].marks",
+    "expect": "Top band (9•10 marks) Comprehensive analysis covering roles, attributes, and qualifications with a well-structured and justified response.\nUpper-mid band (6•8 marks) Good analysis covering most aspects.\nLower-mid band (3•5 marks) Some analysis with partial coverage.\nLower band (1•2 marks) Basic points.\n\nIndicative content:\nRoles to consider:\n• Size of restaurant determines how many levels of the brigade are needed • a small restaurant may combine roles\n• An executive chef sets the menu, manages budgets, and leads the team\n• A sous chef is essential to deputise and supervise during busy service\n• Chefs de partie are needed for each section; commis chefs assist them\n• Kitchen porters keep the kitchen clean and maintain hygiene standards\n\nPersonal attributes:\n• All staff must be hygienic (food safety), hardworking, and able to work under pressure\n• Senior chefs need leadership and communication skills\n• Junior staff need willingness to learn and flexibility\n\nQualifications:\n• Executive and sous chef positions typically require formal training plus years of experience\n• Apprentices bring enthusiasm but need supervision\n• Food hygiene certificates are essential for all kitchen staff\n\nJudgement should weigh the restaurant•s size, budget, cuisine style, and the balance between experienced and trainee staff.",
+    "value": "Top band (9•10 marks) Comprehensive analysis covering roles, attributes, and qualifications with a well-structured and justified response.\nUpper-mid band (6•8 marks) Good analysis covering most aspects.\nLower-mid band (3•5 marks) Some analysis with partial coverage.\nLower band (1•2 marks) Basic points.\n\nIndicative content:\nRoles to consider:\n• Size of restaurant determines how many levels of the brigade are needed • a small restaurant may combine roles\n• An executive chef sets the menu, manages budgets, and leads the team\n• A sous chef is essential to deputise and supervise during busy service\n• Chefs de partie are needed for each section; commis chefs assist them\n• Kitchen porters keep the kitchen clean and maintain hygiene standards\n\nPersonal attributes:\n• All staff must be hygienic (food safety), hardworking, and able to work under pressure\n• Senior chefs need leadership and communication skills\n• Junior staff need willingness to learn and flexibility\n\nQualifications:\n• Executive and sous chef positions typically require formal training plus years of experience\n• Apprentices bring enthusiasm but need supervision\n• All kitchen staff need food hygiene training suitable for their work\n\nJudgement should weigh the restaurant•s size, budget, cuisine style, and the balance between experienced and trainee staff.",
